# ===== Thiqa SaaS fork — begin fork-owned rules =====
# Archives: opaque to git and duplicated by their extracted contents, which are
# tracked (brand/ holds everything distilled from the design handoff).
docs/Thiqa_Group_1_5B_Handoff/*.zip
docs/discovery/**/*.zip

# Bulk discovery output: raw grep dumps, regenerable from the tooling in tools/.
# ~31 MB across three files; the derived findings live in docs/rebranding.md.
docs/discovery/**/react-consumer-search-hits.txt
docs/discovery/**/count-js_innerhtml.txt

# Local stack helper output
SETUP-STATUS.local.md

# Per-tenant branding runtime state, written by skyeagle-branding:materialise.
# This is one tenant's materialised output, not source: on a multi-tenant deployment
# there is one directory per site here. Without this rule a routine `git add -A`
# commits tenant runtime state into the source tree (docs/RebrandingBugs.md RB-04).
interface/modules/custom_modules/oe-module-skyeagle-branding/public/branding/

# Runtime-generated cryptographic key material. These are created by the running
# application, never by a developer, and must never enter version control.
#
#   certificates/*.key                 OAuth2 keypair, written by setupOAuthKeys()
#   logs_and_misc/methods/<version><a|b>   site encryption keys (AES + HMAC pair)
#                                      per OpenEMR\Common\Crypto\KeyVersion
#
# Without these rules the files appear in `git status` as ordinary untracked files,
# indistinguishable from a document someone forgot to add, and a single `git add -A`
# commits live private keys (Phase 2B PB-033/PB-035, RDY-0048).
#
# Scoped deliberately to these two directories. A blanket `*.pem`, `*.key` or
# `sites/**` rule would risk hiding legitimate repository content.
sites/*/documents/certificates/*.key
sites/*/documents/logs_and_misc/methods/*
!sites/*/documents/logs_and_misc/methods/README.md

# Patient document payloads. OpenEMR stores each patient's uploaded documents in a
# directory named for their patient id, so `sites/<site>/documents/1/`, `/2/`, …
# hold the actual file contents.
#
# **This is a production-PHI prevention control, not demo housekeeping.** On this
# instance the payloads are synthetic and marked; on a live instance the same paths
# hold real patient records, and a single `git add -A` would write them into git
# history permanently, where deletion does not remove them.
#
# Deliberately matches only the numeric per-patient directories. The tracked
# structural content of this tree — .htaccess, the category READMEs, the onsite
# portal templates, custom_menus JSON — must stay visible, so the whole
# `sites/*/documents` tree is NOT ignored.
sites/*/documents/[0-9]*/

# Agent probe/verification scripts written to a repo-root scratchpad during a
# session (Phase 2B PB-074, RDY-0048). These are session-local, are regularly
# regenerated, and may carry an instance's paths -- they must never be committed
# by accident.
#
# Evidence harnesses that a PB entry or an EV file cites as "re-runnable" do NOT
# belong here. They live in `docs/evidence/harnesses/` and ARE tracked, because a
# reproduce command pointing at an untracked path is not reproducible by anyone
# but its author.
/scratchpad/
/tmp-phpstan/
# Scratch site directories created while restoring a database snapshot for
# investigation (e.g. sites/rdy0082restore/ from RDY-0082). OpenEMR requires a
# full site skeleton alongside each restored database, so the copy carries a
# sqlconf.php holding that database's credentials plus a fresh OAuth keypair.
#
# The keypair is already covered by the certificates rule above, but sqlconf.php
# is not, and a single `git add -A` would commit live database credentials for
# an instance nobody else can reach.
#
# Named explicitly rather than matched by a `sites/*restore*/` wildcard: sites/
# holds real, tracked deployment content, and a pattern broad enough to catch
# future scratch sites is also broad enough to hide a legitimate one. Add the
# next scratch site by name.
sites/rdy0082restore/

# Word artifacts in the EHI export diagram tree. Graphviz sources there use the
# .dot extension, which Windows also registers to Microsoft Word as a legacy
# template type -- so opening one to read it and saving silently drops a .docx
# beside the real source. Every one of these is an accident; no .docx is tracked
# anywhere in this repository.
Documentation/EHI_Export/docs/diagrams/**/*.docx

# ===== Thiqa SaaS fork — end fork-owned rules =====
